{
  "summary": "This computer looks in good shape: all six findings are low severity and most describe normal setups. The one thing worth a moment of your time is confirming you recognize the second administrator account, <computer>\\<account-2>. After that, glance over the startup apps and the HP dock entry to make sure they match software you actually use.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Administrator accounts on this computer",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291",
        "ACCT-DAILY-ADMIN:44c65344"
      ],
      "explanation": "There are two administrator accounts: your everyday account (<computer>\\<user>) and a second one, <computer>\\<account-2>. Having a separate admin account is actually good practice, so if you created <account-2> yourself, this is fine. If you don't recognize it, take it seriously, because an unknown admin account is a common sign of a break-in. Separately, your everyday account also has admin rights, which means anything you run by mistake could gain full control after a UAC prompt. This is very common on home PCs and is low risk, but using a standard account day to day is safer. The report shows the suggested fix for each."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Apps that start when you sign in (Discord, Spotify, Notion)",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PERSIST-REVIEW:46fde10b",
        "PERSIST-REVIEW:a6556492",
        "PERSIST-REVIEW:422bd707"
      ],
      "explanation": "Three programs start automatically when you sign in: Discord, Spotify and Notion. They run from your personal AppData folder, which programs can write to without admin rights, so the scanner flags them for a quick look. This is how these apps normally install themselves. If you use these apps, there's nothing to worry about. If you don't recognize one, check the report's suggested fix."
    },
    {
      "priority": 3,
      "title": "HP USB-C dock background trigger",
      "finding_ids": [
        "WMI-CONSUMER-REVIEW:7d52aadf"
      ],
      "explanation": "A WMI event subscription, a Windows feature that can run a program automatically when certain events happen, launches the HP USB-C Dock G5 firmware installer from Program Files. Only administrators can place files there, and the scanner found no suspicious traits. If you use an HP USB-C dock, this is expected hardware software. If you've never had an HP dock, it's worth a closer look using the report's guidance."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
